A||||15||DR650SE||Clutchless shifting...Is is bad?||||5||
Z||000000||DR650SE||11-18-2001||09:17 PM||||Just wondering if this is bad.  When I do it it's a smooth shift and no gear grinding going on.  I was just wondering if it was bad for the car and how so.  If not then does anyone else do it?  I juyst get lazy and don't always use the clutch.  I don't force it either.  Its smooth.<P>------------------<BR>AIM:  DR650SE<BR>2 Black 86 SE's V-6 4-SPD||||reg||5
Z||000001||PBJ||11-18-2001||09:29 PM||||instead of wearing the clutch you are wearing the the transmission syncro(s)<P>I would use the clutch.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||artherd||11-18-2001||09:34 PM||||With a Syncromesh, helical-cut gearbox (read: about all street trannys.)  you have to *really hit* those shift points  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I mean, +-10rpm or so.  <P>I've done it occationally, but I would *not* make a habbit of it. (at the most, once a week or so. That, a gearbox full of Red Line MTL, and a lot of tallent, will keep you out of trouble.)<P>Keep in mind that our boxes really weren't meant to be shifted this way.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000003||MrPBody||11-18-2001||09:43 PM||||True; however, it's a useful skill when your clutch hydraulics die.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||11-18-2001||09:52 PM||||I do it all the time!! (but then, I have an automatic!)||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroMaster88||11-18-2001||10:05 PM||||Did it for about 2 weeks while waiting on a petal.  Didnt hurt the trans any.  Just dont do it all the time!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">                   <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||1
Z||000006||DR650SE||11-18-2001||10:29 PM||||o ok thanks for the info, good to know, good to know||||reg||11
Z||000007||Tom88gt||11-18-2001||11:49 PM||||It's called "floating" in the truck driving world.  It does no damage.  Just be easy with it 'cause no matter how hard you try, it's not gonna go into gear unless the engine's speed and the car's speed are right for the gear your trying to go to.  When it's all lined up the shifter is almost "sucked" into gear.  Otherwise it's just gonna make that awful grinding sound until the speeds match up.<P>||||reg||14
Z||000008||Gerald||11-19-2001||12:52 AM||||I dont have troble getting out of gear. Its getting back in that makes funny noises. BTW any one wanna buy a 87 coupe?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||11-19-2001||02:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tom88gt:<BR><B>It's called "floating" in the truck driving world.  It does no damage.  Just be easy with it 'cause no matter how hard you try, it's not gonna go into gear unless the engine's speed and the car's speed are right for the gear your trying to go to.  When it's all lined up the shifter is almost "sucked" into gear.  Otherwise it's just gonna make that awful grinding sound until the speeds match up.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>A truck tranny isn't synchronized.  You either float the gears in a big truck or you double clutch.  Big truck trannies are meant to work this way.  Automotive trannies are not!!  Don't float the gears in a car!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||11-19-2001||08:01 AM||||the sloppy cable shifting cables make it tough. Lots easier with a normal cars transmission. I do it occasionally with mine but you DO have to hit rpms perfect. Regular cars with rwd like camaros are piece of cake. Downshifts are a little trickier than upshifts. The synchronizers are working anyway so unless you grind the gears, I see no harm.||||reg||1
Z||000011||BOILERMAKER||11-19-2001||10:49 AM||||I agree with Roger.  No harm unless you grind.  If you hit it right the first time everytime all your are doing is saving your clutch.||||reg||1
Z||000012||DR650SE||11-19-2001||02:45 PM||||When I do "float" the gears I always hit the rpm perfectly.  Upshifting and down shifting.  It doesn't feel hard on anything, It just slides right into gear.  Anyother opinions or facts?  Anyone ever damage the syncros this way?||||reg||1
Z||000013||recluse||11-19-2001||03:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MrPBody:<BR><B>True; however, it's a useful skill when your clutch hydraulics die.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Very true.  But whats even worse is taking off with no clutch from stops.  Thats GOTTA be hard on the starter.<P>||||reg||1
